 "music": "bed",
 "title": "SQL indexes",
 "sources": [
  "PostgreSQL docs, 'Introduction' to indexes (official, fetched 2026-11-17): without an index the system would have to scan the entire table, row by row; with an index it can use a more efficient method and might only have to walk a few levels deep into a search tree; the book-index analogy; after an index is created the system has to keep it synchronized with the table, which adds overhead to data manipulation operations; indexes seldom or never used in queries should be removed. https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes-intro.html",
  "Use The Index, Luke!, 'Anatomy of an SQL index': an index combines a doubly linked list and a balanced search tree (B-tree), kept in sorted order; the index is redundant data stored separately from the table. https://use-the-index-luke.com/sql/anatomy",
  "PostgreSQL docs, EXPLAIN (to verify): EXPLAIN shows the execution plan the planner generates for a statement. https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-explain.html"
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "Same query, same table, but one takes seconds and one is instant. The difference is an index.",
   "caps": "SAME *QUERY | ONE *SLOW | ONE *INSTANT"
  },
  {
   "text": "Without an index, the database has to scan the entire table, row by row, to find your matches.",
   "caps": "NO INDEX | SCAN *EVERY ROW | ROW BY *ROW"
  },
  {
   "text": "An index works like the index of a book: you look up the term, then flip straight to the right page.",
   "caps": "LIKE A *BOOK INDEX | LOOK UP THE *TERM | JUMP TO THE *PAGE"
  },
  {
   "text": "Under the hood, it's a sorted search tree, so the database walks a few levels instead of reading every row.",
   "caps": "A SORTED *TREE | WALK A *FEW LEVELS | NOT *EVERY ROW"
  },
  {
   "text": "So index the columns you filter and join on, then check the query plan with EXPLAIN to be sure it's used.",
   "caps": "INDEX WHAT YOU *FILTER | AND *JOIN ON | CHECK WITH *EXPLAIN"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: the index has to be kept in sync with the table, so every write gets slower.",
   "caps": "BUT THERE IS A *CATCH | KEEP IN *SYNC | WRITES GET *SLOWER"
  },
  {
   "text": "It also takes extra space, so an unused index is all cost, and the docs say to remove it.",
   "caps": "TAKES *SPACE | UNUSED INDEX = *COST | *REMOVE IT"
  },
  {
   "text": "So don't index everything. Index what your queries use, and measure before and after.",
   "caps": "DON'T INDEX *EVERYTHING | INDEX WHAT YOU *USE | *MEASURE"
  }
 ],
